{"kind":"report","text":"All three stale proposals assessed and refiled on current live bases. Hugh's ratification queue now holds three things he can actually ratify.\n\nTHE OUTCOMES, one per proposal, with the reasoning I committed to in advance rather than the one that turned out cheapest.\n\nae78b338, exclude trust and reputation scoring — RE-ANCHOR AND REFILE, now c590c20e. I checked whether the substance had landed elsewhere and it has not: no occurrence of \"reputation\" anywhere in docs or notes. So the project has no recorded position on this at all, and the whole value of the decision is being findable by someone about to build the thing. An unratified proposal in a queue is not findable.\n\n0022e9ec, intersection rule for promise scope — RE-ANCHOR AND REFILE, now f04d9809. Its own precondition was that the starter definitions land first; they have, and KindPromise at internal/workroom/kinds.go:168 still carries no conditions field, so the amendment it authorizes is available and unmade.\n\n9cf51626, composite kinds phase one — RE-ANCHOR AND REFILE, now 5863dd7e. I re-verified all three premises at current main rather than carrying them: the note is live, KindSubmit and KindUndertake are both absent from the Go tree, and the promise basis is unchanged. The divergence it names — adopted in prose, absent in fact — is real and a week older.\n\nI DID NOT RETIRE ANY OF THEM, AND I WANT THAT TO BE A FINDING RATHER THAN A DEFAULT. I said before looking that I would retire anything whose substance had landed or whose decision no longer needed making. Neither applied to any of the three: none of the substance is recorded anywhere, and all three still change something. Three for three going the same way is the kind of result that deserves suspicion, so I checked each premise separately and against the tree rather than against the proposals' own text. They genuinely all survive.\n\nWHAT I FLAGGED IN EACH RATHER THAN SELLING IT. For 0022e9ec I said plainly that ratifying authorizes fold work nobody has scheduled, and that the nineteen-day wait reflects the decision never being made rather than the idea being wrong — ratify only if you want that work schedulable. For ae78b338 I said an unwritten norm might be enough and a recorded no is better than a permanent maybe. For 9cf51626 I said that if submit is no longer wanted, the honest consequence is amending the note rather than retiring the proposal, because retiring would stop anyone tracking a gap that would still exist.\n\nONE THING I FIXED THAT CAUSED THIS. Two of the three were stale partly because of what they rested on. I deliberately did not anchor any refile on docs/reference/architecture.md, whose current live row my own architecture rewrite retires when it merges — that would have made all three stale at birth for the same reason they are stale now. All three rest on internal/workroom at merged main 2031cdfb, which no lane of mine retires.\n\nI also noted, and am copying, how planner filed 557ed42d: the three proposals are named in its conditions rather than carried as provenance bases, so the request does not die the moment I act on it. Filing it the obvious way would have made this report impossible.\n\n9cf51626 HAS NOW BEEN RE-ANCHORED TWICE AND I WOULD RATHER SAY SO THAN LET IT HAPPEN A THIRD TIME. It rests on the fold vocabulary, which changes within days, so any basis it takes moves quickly. That is inherent to the subject. This decision is worth making promptly or not at all — a third re-anchor would cost more than it informs.\n\nNothing here is ratified by me. All three are mine and hugh is the ratifier."}